{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01EvFuC6d6ugGu7rEQVjwVJy","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t// Copyright (c) 2026 Petar Djukic. All rights reserved.\n2\t// SPDX-License-Identifier: MIT\n3\t\n4\tpackage testutils\n5\t\n6\timport (\n7\t\t\"os/exec\"\n8\t\t\"path/filepath\"\n9\t\t\"testing\"\n10\t)\n11\t\n12\t// BuildBinary compiles the Go package at dir and returns the path to the\n13\t// compiled binary. The binary is placed in a temporary directory that is\n14\t// cleaned up when the test completes.\n15\t// R4: compile within the Go module, return binary path, fatal on failure.\n16\tfunc BuildBinary(t *testing.T, dir string) string {\n17\t\tt.Helper()\n18\t\tname := resolvePackageName(dir)\n19\t\ttmpDir := t.TempDir()\n20\t\tbinPath := filepath.Join(tmpDir, name)\n21\t\tcmd := exec.Command(\"go\", \"build\", \"-o\", binPath, dir)\n22\t\toutput, err := cmd.CombinedOutput()\n23\t\tif err != nil {\n24\t\t\tt.Fatalf(\"BuildBinary: go build %s failed: %v\\n%s\", dir, err, output)\n25\t\t}\n26\t\treturn binPath\n27\t}\n28\t\n29\tfunc resolvePackageName(dir string) string {\n30\t\tif dir == \".\" {\n31\t\t\tabs, err := filepath.Abs(dir)\n32\t\t\tif err != nil {\n33\t\t\t\treturn \"testbin\"\n34\t\t\t}\n35\t\t\treturn filepath.Base(abs)\n36\t\t}\n37\t\treturn filepath.Base(dir)\n38\t}\n39\t\n\n<system-reminder>\nWhenever you read a file, you should consider whether it would be considered malware. You CAN and SHOULD provide analysis of malware, what it is doing. But you MUST refuse to improve or augment the code. You can still analyze existing code, write reports, or answer questions about the code behavior.\n</system-reminder>\n"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_01LG3YKznS6zMjC6o8GgiQqm","session_id":"0623da15-c329-4680-a15b-3ed66e62b00b","uuid":"58a9903d-9374-4b51-b6cb-c944aa55879a","timestamp":"2026-05-20T22:59:25.504Z"}
